                        "entry_id": "b6203031-2250-3f28-ae39-d401c547c957",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The state of being tangent; an instance of (something) being tangent."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7aeb57b0-4b03-4867-8931-15015b53fc3a",
                                "sense_id": "4047a480-17a8-3e41-a9b0-12e1e585bea9",
                                "text": "In this section we will apply the concept of connectedness in dimension d to study tangencies of algebraic varieties."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5157be4-e720-454e-b5af-8e5cbccf5848",
                                "sense_id": "4047a480-17a8-3e41-a9b0-12e1e585bea9",
                                "text": "To be tangent to a circle or arc, a line must touch the circle or arc at only one point, and a line drawn from the center of the circle or arc must be perpendicular to the tangent line at the point of tangency. (See Figure 6.82.)"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c606f275-cb67-40eb-8474-e568b0d1cc26",
                                "sense_id": "4047a480-17a8-3e41-a9b0-12e1e585bea9",
                                "text": "If the original tangency is at A, then the new tangency cannot be at O or P, as either possibility would require two indifference curves to cross.[…]Instead, the new tangency is at a point like B."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
